/* An existential witness chosen from the recorded copy that the dwf reduction adds.
   The predicate P of Tsukada, Unno, Padon and Shoham, "A Primal-Dual Perspective on
   Program Verification Algorithms" (POPL 2025; arXiv:2501.01512v1, Example 7.3),
   with the witness range made explicit (0 <= z <= 1, so the optimizer cannot erase
   the quantifier into a nullary predicate) and a nu-conjunct R that the refuter has
   to work for (see "why R" below).

   Verdict: INVALID.  R is false: its greatest fixpoint needs y - k > 0 for every k.
   So the approximants of P are P^0 = false, P^(k+1)(x) = exists z in {0,1}. P^k(z)
   \/ false = false, and the least fixpoint is empty: P 0 is false.  In game terms
   every play of the prover stays in P forever, visiting only 0 and 1, and P is a
   least fixpoint.

   The added parameter.  With use_dwf the reduction replaces the mu-predicate P(x)
   by a nu-predicate P'(r, x), r a copy of an earlier visited argument, and each
   recursive call P(z) by  P'(z, z) /\ P'(r, z) /\ WF_P(r, z)  (restart the record,
   keep the record, and the record must dominate the new argument under the
   disjunctively well-founded relation WF_P).  The query P 0 becomes P'(0, 0).

   The unsound strategy.  z := (if r = 0 then 1 else 0) -- the witness reads the
   record r -- with WF_P the union of  max(a,0) > max(b,0)  and
   max(3-a,0) > max(3-b,0),  each a ranking into the naturals, so the union is
   disjunctively well-founded.  Every reachable record is 0 or 1; from r = 1 the
   witness 0 satisfies the first relation, from r = 0 the witness 1 the second, so
   P' = true is a post-fixpoint and the transformed problem is VALID.  The real
   play is 0, 1, 0, 1, ...: the branch that keeps the record 0 meets 0 again and
   needs (0,0) in WF_P, which no well-founded relation contains.  The strategy wins
   because the two branches at one real position see different records and make
   different choices, which no single play can do.

   An honest strategy (witness a function of x only) loses the transformed game:
   from 0 the witnesses stay in {0,1}, some value v repeats, and the record-keeping
   branch from the first v needs (v,v) in WF_P.  An implementation that lets the
   witness see r answers "valid" here, which is wrong.

   Where today's MuStrat leaks r although its Skolem predicate FN_P_z takes x
   only (argued from the code, not measured): a strategy is a finite TERM SET and
   the check substitutes each term into the clause and joins the results with
   "or", so the choice AMONG the terms {z = 0, z = 1} is made by the CHC solver's
   invariant for P'(r, x), which sees r.  The two constant terms plus the two
   rankings above are therefore enough to reproduce the cheat.

   Why R.  Without it the dual (a pure nu-problem with no strategy to find) wins at
   its first check, so the cheat is never given a chance; R's dual is a
   mu-predicate whose ranking the refuter must first synthesise. */
P 0
s.t.
P (x: int): bool =mu (exists (z: int). 0 <= z /\ z <= 1 /\ P z) \/ R x;
R (y: int): bool =nu y > 0 /\ R (y - 1);
